WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.762
Strong detection rules often begin with a behavioral question.

2
00:00:03.762 --> 00:00:06.356
What activity are we trying to identify?

3
00:00:06.356 --> 00:00:08.801
What suspicious behavior concerns us?

4
00:00:08.801 --> 00:00:13.161
What risk would the organization face if this behavior occurred and went unnoticed?

5
00:00:13.161 --> 00:00:16.963
Behavior-first thinking helps teams avoid random rule creation.

6
00:00:16.963 --> 00:00:27.380
Instead of starting with a log field and looking for something to match, detection engineers start with a meaningful defensive objective and then determine what evidence could support it.

7
00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:42.785
Useful inputs include threat intelligence, prior incidents, ATT&CK technique mapping, organizational priorities, red team lessons, compliance concerns, and patterns analysts have observed during investigations.

8
00:00:42.785 --> 00:00:46.736
These inputs help keep the rule connected to real operational need.

9
00:00:46.736 --> 00:00:47.924
Context matters.

10
00:00:47.924 --> 00:00:52.164
The same behavior may be normal in one environment and suspicious in another.

11
00:00:52.164 --> 00:01:02.391
A good detection idea considers who performs the activity, where it happens, how often it is expected, and what surrounding events would make it more or less concerning.
